Your concern is grounded in a material difference between the offers. Vanguard is asking you to accept without knowing your team, manager, technical scope, or possibly location and working model. Kestrel has specified those terms and introduced you to the engineering lead.

The 4 months of interviewing should not determine the decision—it is a sunk cost—but the late discovery of Vanguard’s placement process is relevant because it limits your ability to evaluate the role before signing.

Before deciding, ask Vanguard in writing for:

- teams you could be assigned to and likely probabilities;
- the placement criteria and who controls the decision;
- whether you can reject a placement or defer your start;
- examples of recent hires’ preferences versus actual placements;
- whether title, location, remote status, or responsibilities can change with placement;
- a pre-signing meeting with likely managers.

If Vanguard will not provide meaningful constraints or a placement guarantee, treat its offer as an offer to join the company, not an offer for a defined job. With essentially equal compensation, Kestrel offers materially lower role and manager risk. Unless Vanguard’s brand, scale, or internal mobility has specific value that outweighs that uncertainty for you, I would choose Kestrel.
